ond wave has been catastrophic  images of people gasping for breath  and collapsing in front of hospitals  where no beds or oxygen are available  have impacted the mental well-being of  many  some experts are convinced this wave has  triggered a mental health epidemic of  its own  we can now speak to dr samir parrik who  joins us from gurugram near delhi he  leads the department  of mental health and behavioral sciences  at fortis  healthcare thanks for joining us we've  just been hearing from indians  particularly young indians talking about  their struggles with their mental health  during this pandemic  is the health care provision there for  people to seek help  two aspects to this uh in  any case mental health prevalence if you  look worldwide  right depression single largest illness  300 million people globally suffering  from it  we in any case have more than 500  shortage of mental experts in our  country  that is pre-coped now what we need to  understand is  that yes more conversations will be  happening around mental health  lot more than four people are talking  about mental health  so mental health services or provisions  are not merely about experts and  expertization when  it comes to our part of the world it  will also involve  the family support friends social media  communicating with your primary  physicians and at  multiple levels the kind of psychosocial  support and guidance  that's what we are working with right  now  you mentioned help coming i suppose from  within communities rather than  necessarily from  healthcare professionals but one of the  things about india is that it's  enormously  diverse does that make it challenging to  ensure that all groups are getting the  sort of help that they need  but that's an obvious i mean  even if you look at it and again i i  need to use the pre-covered example  because it gives the context to it  that we largely have most experts  of belonging to the mental health  fraternity in urban india  and as yo